it's november 11th and in one week a  copy of the us constitution is going up  for auction everyone expects it to draw  the usual crowd of wealthy collectors  but the crypto community has another  plan they want to crowdfund enough money  to place a winning bid and they'll do it  using a dow  there's only one week until the auction  so they need to move fast on friday the  12th they put up a website things are  messy with competing code repositories  but on saturday they find some partners  to help juicebox will help with  crowdfunding syndicate dao will help  with legal and ftx will help with  exchange the auction house agrees to  work with the dow on sunday but there's  a problem sotheby's has to follow know  your customer rules so the dow needs to  partner with an llc two members of the  dao step up to form the llc and bridge  the crypto and traditional finance  worlds it's now monday and things are  really picking up steam ten thousand  people are chatting in the discord the  media starts catching on [ __ ] mccormick  writes a blog then cnbc covers it  donations start flowing and they're out  to buy the united states constitution  tuesday leads to more big names signing  on chris dixon and other a16z folks are  now supporting the project on wednesday  they break 20 million dollars raised  starts looking like this might actually  happen thursday is auction day and  people are absolutely stoked the dow has  over 40 million dollars now it's chaos  in the discord no one knows who's  representing the dao clearly both  bidders really want this brook lampley  places the winning bid at 41 million  but no one knows who she's bidding for  sold 41 million dow backers are claiming  victory but slowly the truth leaks out  the dow lost and the winner is revealed  it's ken griffin the founder of citadel  an arch nemesis of the wall street bets  reddit a crushing defeat  the story of the constitution dao is  useful because it touches on so many  different aspects of the current web 3  landscape so let's go through the good  parts of this then talk about some of  the bad parts and finally close on the  ugly because it gets really ugly here  first up is the good well it's the  constitution what's not to like it's an  extremely meaningful historical document  and the dow only had two rules for what  they were going to do with it it had to  be displayed publicly and it had to have  a plaque next to it that mentioned the  constitution dao's website that's a  noble goal and it's a big part of what  led to the massive surge in support the  team also did a great job of making it  clear that they weren't in this for  financial gain even though they could  have easily given themselves some tokens  for free at the start of the project  they didn't which helped build trust in  the community now to be fair there's  still a world where the core  contributors wind up benefiting from the  project the team was planning on  submitting a proposal for compensation  after the auction that would have been  voted on by the dao but that certainly  seems reasonable to me and a lot of the  core team members had other web 3  projects and a high profile story like  this could draw attention to what  they're working on but again that's  totally reasonable this approach led to  a very positive community developing  around the project although i didn't  contribute to the project i was in the  discord and could see that everyone was  just having fun and excited to be a part  of a cool story no one was trying to  inflate expectations and the core team  was very clear that the dow was merely a  governance token and did not represent  real ownership in the constitution [ __ ]  mccormick put it really well in his blog  post when he said if you want to  contribute you should think about it as  purchasing a keepsake as opposed to  investing in an asset even with this  caveat i think the fact that the money  donated to the constitution dao was  going to fund the purchase of a real  physical item is important here unlike  previous crypto projects the  constitution dao is very easy to  understand even for people who are new  to crypto at this point most people  understand what an nft is they've seen  the crazy jpegs selling for millions of  dollars and heard about massive  sotheby's auctions but not everyone  agrees that these nfts have value the  constitution on the other hand has  centuries of history behind it and could  actually generate cash flow at least in  theory although this wasn't the plan  some museums do charge entrance fees so  it's not crazy to come up with some sort  of fundamental value based on  traditional financial modeling even  though the project didn't pan out as  planned it definitely served as a good  educational tool for sparking interest  in dows the idea of a decentralized  autonomous organization is still very  new to most people but the constitution  dao helped spread the idea that lots of  people could come together quickly  online to crowdfund a specific goal and  unlike traditional crowdfunding taos  have a built-in voting mechanism it's  still unclear where these dows will go  or how useful they'll be in the future  but more people are aware of how they  work and i think that's a win for the  team behind this project obviously the  hope here was to win the auction and  propel the dow into the mainstream  zeitgeist and it doesn't look like  that'll happen but it's still remarkable  that the project was able to break into  the mainstream media in just a few days  crypto broadly has been a hot topic for  years now but most of the attention  comes back to price movements every time  bitcoin hits a new all-time high there  will undoubtedly be a press cycle  covering it and that's fine if you own  bitcoin but i don't think it really  moves the needle where it's needed most  the developer community this project  represents a step forward precisely  because there wasn't an expectation of  massive overnight gain so one of the few  ways to get more involved was to  actually contribute to the project in  some way if you're a proponent of crypto  this seems like a good development the  constitution dao was basically a massive  stress test of the current web 3  infrastructure and it clearly exposed a  bunch of areas for development most of  the problems which we'll get to in a  minute do have solutions and they are  now much easier to see for interested  developers last but certainly not least  it is extremely good that no money was  stolen here crypto is still very much a  wild west and fortunes are made and lost  every day the very first dao which was  appropriately named just the dao raised  150 million dollars before quickly  getting hacked back in 2016. even though  web3 project can offer very high levels  of security in theory there's still a  ton of bad actors out there who can scam  people but that didn't happen here even  though they lost the auction supporters  will get their money back and that's a  good result all things considered the  stakes were clearly pretty high with  tens of millions of dollars flowing into  the dow but the team handled things  responsibly and kept communication  flowing but now we have to turn to the  bad side of this project and we should  probably start with the actual structure  of the dao because it's extremely  convoluted web 3 projects tend to work  best when they are off in their own  little pristine walled gardens the world  of traditional finance is extremely  messy and highly regulated but wading  through all of that was unavoidable for  this project let me walk you through how  money was actually going to flow from  individuals to the original owner of the  constitution who was selling it step one  a contributor goes to a cryptocurrency  exchange like coinbase and buys ether  with us dollars from their bank account  step two they now send that ether from  coinbase to a wallet like metamask step  3 they use metamask to send ether to the  constitution dao project which is hosted  by a crowdfunding platform called  juicebox step 4 all of the ether  donations are then collected in a  multi-sig wallet which requires  agreement from 13 different team members  in order to move that money further step  5 because sotheby's can't work directly  with the dao a delaware llc with just  two signatories has to interface with  the auction house step six the llc would  orchestrate the transfer to sotheby's in  the event that they did win the auction  that's a remarkably complex process for  something that's pretty simple at its  core we're just talking about a bunch of  people pooling their resources to buy a  single object this shouldn't be so  complicated and yet it is because web 3  is so new and best practices are still  being developed on the fly one of the  big benefits of crypto projects is  decentralization and the constitution  dao was going to allow everyone who  donated to vote on where to display the  constitution in proportion to the amount  that they paid the tokens they issued  would allow these votes to take place  without needing any single person to be  trusted to count the votes this is a  great use for crypto and it wouldn't  have any issues if there wasn't a  physical asset involved the problem is  that the further you get from the  blockchain the more centralization is  required to move things forward quickly  and at a certain point the constitution  dao wound up in a situation where you  basically had to trust that the two  people responsible for the delaware llc  would just do the right thing i think  these issues can be solved though  especially with a long enough time  horizon it's remarkable that the team  was able to do as much as they did in  just seven days and i really have no  doubt that we'll see much stronger  patterns develop in the next few years  here but we still need to talk about the  most obvious bad outcome here they lost  the dao originally thought that raising  20 million dollars would be enough since  that's how much experts thought this  copy of the constitution was worth they  wound up raising more than twice that  much but they still couldn't win and a  big reason why is because the funding  total was public given that the maximum  possible bid was known to everyone the  seller of the constitution meaning the  person who owned it and put it up for  auction could bid against the dow  directly to drive up the price if the  seller knows that the dao is willing to  pay up to 40 million dollars they will  just bid up to 39 million dollars to get  the maximum payday from the dow even if  the dow does win the auction in that  scenario the more money they raise the  more expensive the final price gets now  to be fair it doesn't look like the  seller was bidding on the constitution  themselves ken griffin is just a  multi-billionaire and was able to outbid  the dow directly but it's always a bad  idea to show your cards before you have  to and that's exactly what the  constitution dow did here even still in  the long term there could be a solution  to this problem there is a branch of  crypto called zero knowledge proofs that  could obfuscate the exact amount of  money raised by the dao while still  giving sotheby's confirmation that the  dao could fund their current bid it's  complex stuff and it's not going to be  implemented next week but it's  theoretically possible and it would make  a lot of sense for the next project of  this kind the last bad outcome from this  project has been a bit of a punchline on  twitter transaction fees because of all  the interest in web3 projects right now  the ethereum blockchain has become very  expensive to work with every time you go  to execute a transaction you'll usually  get hit with a gas fee somewhere between  twenty and a hundred dollars even though  the dow lost the auction everyone that  contributed still had to pay gas fees to  transfer their ether into the dow some  people were estimating that the total  cost of transaction fees could be as  high as 5 million dollars but it looks  like it was closer to 880k that's still  a ton of money though and it doesn't  really fit with the long-term promise of  cryptocurrency worse still everyone who  put money in is now going to be hit with  gas fees on the way out as they request  refunds the median contribution size was  only 200  so lots of people wound up paying nearly  50 in transaction fees everyone in the  crypto community is aware of this  problem though and they know that it  will hurt adoption if it's not fixed the  ethereum team is working on eth 2.0 to  lower these fees and other blockchains  like solana already offer low fees and  similar functionality so transaction  fees are unlikely to be a major problem  in the long term even if they won't be  resolved overnight but now we have to  discuss the really ugly truth about this  project the core concept here is  remarkably simple we're just talking  about a bunch of people chipping in to  buy something expensive it's not that  complicated and yet there still doesn't  seem to be an easy way to get this done  why is that part of this has to do with  trade-offs there are good reasons for  know your customer and anti-money  laundering laws and it's also important  to protect people from scams but it  seems ridiculous to me that it's still  so difficult in the year 2021. we're not  talking about curing cancer here or  building a mars colony it's really just  moving some numbers around that  represent value when i think about the  future i still envision all the classic  sci-fi elements flying cars interstellar  travel immersive virtual environments  these crypto projects are a stark  reminder that we still have a remarkably  messy financial system and dealing with  that just takes time i do think this is  important work and i think that the  constitution dow could lead to some  meaningful innovation but it's still  just a bit sad that we have to spend so  much time untangling messy systems just  to move forward i'm definitely going to  do more research on crypto and web3 so  if you'd like to follow along just drop  your email at johncoogan.com and please  check out this recommended video the  youtube algorithm thinks you'll really  like it

